#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148789 :tutorial/content "We can find a percentage part of a number using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $16 \\%$ of $300.$\n\nHere, the whole is $300$ and the percentage is $16 \\% .$ So, the part is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = 16\\% \\times 300.\n$$\n\nBut how do we multiply a percentage by a number? We have two methods of doing this. Let's apply both and check that they give the same answer.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n16\\% = 16 \\div 100 = 0.16\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =16%\\times 300 \\\\  & =0.16\\times 300 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\%$ of $300$ is $48.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n16\\% = \\dfrac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =16%\\times 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{100}\\times 300 \\\\  & =16\\times \\frac{300}{100} \\\\  & =16\\times 3 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16 \\%$ of $300$ is $48.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41c3207b-f9a6-5e36-9a0b-a6ad0c9e8a18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8198 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
